---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import FileWithCornerstone from "../../components/FileWithCornerstone.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="What NMLS is — and what it is not"
  description="The Nationwide Multistate Licensing System is the filing system for many state financial licenses. It does not issue licenses. Here is who files what."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="lms-crumb"><a href="/guides">Guides</a> / NMLS</p>
      <h1>NMLS, in plain English</h1>
      <p>The system of record for many state licenses. Not a national license, and not the agency that says yes or no.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container article" style="max-width:720px;">
      <h2>What it is</h2>
      <p>
        The Nationwide Multistate Licensing System (NMLS) is a web system run for state
        regulators through the Conference of State Bank Supervisors. Companies and
        individuals use it to apply for, amend, renew, and surrender license authorities
        that a state has chosen to manage on the system. Mortgage company and mortgage
        loan originator licenses are on NMLS in every state because the SAFE Act put
        them there. Many states have since added money-transmitter, consumer-finance,
        and some collection licenses to the same record.
      </p>

      <h2>What it is not</h2>
      <p>
        NMLS does not issue licenses. The state agency still reviews the file and
        grants or denies the authority. NMLS also does not replace a state’s own
        documents, surety-bond forms, or net-worth tests. A company can hold several
        state licenses on one NMLS ID and still have a different checklist in each state.
      </p>

      <h2>Who files what</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Companies</strong> file Form MU1 (the company record) and Form MU2 for
        each control person. <strong>Mortgage loan originators</strong> file Form MU4
        and must be sponsored by a licensed company. Branches, where a state requires
        them, are a separate request on the same company record.
      </p>
      <p>
        Money transmitters typically add a money-services license on that same MU1,
        then upload a BSA/AML program and proof of the surety bond each state wants.
        Consumer lenders do the same with the state’s consumer-finance or supervised-loan
        authority. Collection agencies file on NMLS only in states that have moved that
        license onto the system — others still use a board or state portal.
      </p>

      <h2>Federal registry versus state license</h2>
      <p>
        Employees of federally regulated banks and credit unions who originate
        residential mortgages register on the NMLS federal registry. That registration
        is not a state MLO license. Non-depository mortgage companies and their
        originators still need the state licenses.
      </p>

      <h2>How the public checks a license</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="https://www.nmlsconsumeraccess.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NMLS Consumer Access</a>
        is the free public search. It shows companies and individuals licensed or
        registered through NMLS. It does not list every collection agency or every
        state-portal license. If a firm is missing, check whether that regulator
        actually manages the license on NMLS.
      </p>

      <h2>Where this site sends you next</h2>
      <p>
        For who needs the license and which agency takes the filing, use the
        <a href="/directory">directory</a> and the
        <a href="/states">state index</a>. For the filing itself,
        <a href="https://cornerstonelicensing.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cornerstone Licensing</a>
        prepares and submits the NMLS record.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:720px;">
      <FileWithCornerstone topic="an NMLS application" />
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .article h2 { font-size: 1.2rem; margin-top: 32px; margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .article h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .article p { font-size: 0.93rem; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 14px; }
</style>
